{"status": "ok", "message-type": "work-list", "message-version": "1.0.0", "message": {"facets": {}, "total-results": 11651484, "items": [{"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 7, 29]], "date-time": "2026-07-29T07:58:29Z", "timestamp": 1785311909946, "version": "3.55.0"}, "posted": {"date-parts": [[2026]]}, "group-title": "SSRN", "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Elsevier BV", "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2026, 1, 1]], "date-time": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1767225600000}, "content-version": "unspecified", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://www.uspto.gov/ip-policy/copyright-policy/copyright-basics"}], "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "abstract": "<jats:p>The rapid development of sophisticated Large Language Model (LLM) workflows, including agentic systems, multi-step reasoning pipelines, and tool-integrated approaches, has led to impressive reported performance across various benchmarks. However, we argue that the field is repeating a critical mistake from early machine learning: reporting results on data that has been implicitly used for training or tuning LLM-based workflows and pipelines. The complexity of modern LLM workflows obscures the fact that iterative prompt engineering, benchmark-driven development, and workflow refinement constitute a form of training on evaluation data. This position paper draws parallels to historical overfitting practices in Machine Learning and shows how current LLM development methodologies systematically conflate training and testing data. It then proposes best practices to address this growing methodological crisis before it undermines the credibility of AI research in both scientific and commercial applications.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.2139/ssrn.7117159", "type": "posted-content", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 7, 29]], "date-time": "2026-07-29T06:59:06Z", "timestamp": 1785308346000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["The Overfitting Crisis in LLM W